             :reading "The pattern's core (When a rented multi-GPU box runs a job that fits on ONE device, and the run is really several INDEPENDENT sub-runs (ablation arms, seeds, hyperparameter points), do not run them sequentially on GPU 0 while GPUs 1..k sit idle. Pin each independent run to its own device (`CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=i`) and launch them concurrently — the box finishes all arms in roughly one arm's wall-clock instead of the sum. And ask the prior question first: if the model only needs one GPU, a 4-GPU box is only worth renting BECAUSE you will fan the arms across it — otherwise right-size down to a 1-GPU box. This is the training-run sibling of saturate-the-accelerator-with-concurrency: there the unit of parallelism is concurrent requests on one card; here it is concurrent independent runs across cards.) at agent grain: when work splits into independent lanes, provision an idle executor per lane rather than serialising. The condition 'if you need multiple lanes' is the guard's need; 'you can also use kimi-3' is the produced availability. The GPU context is incidental."
